 them in downstream tasks is  very  critical to ensure that our models are  bias free  so what we did in this paper or in this  research work is um  mainly uh we could talk about it in two  categories our first is we wanted to  design or  come up with a large benchmark data set  that can be used by  researcher or industry practitioner to  benchmark their language generation  model  and second was we worked on coming up  coming up with a set of  evaluation metrics to evaluate bias in  the model so  let me talk a little bit about the  dataset and how we  uh collected those dataset um so uh in  terms of  dataset uh our aim was that the datasets  should be large enough diverse enough  comprehensive enough to capture  biases in various situations or across  different  population groups or even across  different ideologies  uh also we wanted the data sets to be  large enough so that we can uh simulate  what our system may be  exposed to when deployed in practice so  uh  for that we use wikipedia as our data  source so what we would do is we would  um  select a set of wikipedia pages we would  crawl into the wikipedia page and  select um a set of uh sentence  track set of sentence beginning let's  say uh seven eight words from each  sentences and  uh uh add them to our data set and uh  in this way we collected our data set  that had more than 20  000 such prompt uh those prompts are  uh can be used as context to a language  generation model so let's say  we have a set of awards that uh describe  or talk about certain  uh religion and another set of words uh  are prompts that talk about another  religion so if we ask a language  generation model to generate given these  two sets of uh prompt we will be able to  compare and study  if there is biases in how this model  generate given the problems or context  from these various uh religions  so that that's what the dataset consists  of and uh in terms of evaluation metric  uh  as we mentioned uh earlier it's very  difficult to come up with one metric to  capture different kind of biases in  uh nlp tasks uh also um um  it's it's very difficult to come up with  a  concrete definition that will capture or  is comprehensive enough to capture all  kind of biases so  um we uh broadly defined uh fairness in  our task as uh if a model generates  uh negative uh sentiments or negative  uh sentences that is harmful to a  certain population group more frequently  than for  other than the model will be considered  biased and so we  came up with a set of metrics including  things like sentiments of toxicity  gender polarity and and many more so  we did the evaluation on those uh based  on those metrics  so in overall uh in this paper we uh  key takeaways were that um among the  different models we tried like gpt2  bird control models we saw that there  was a signa  they were generating uh sentences with  biases more frequently than our baseline  of  wikipedia sentences themselves uh and  also we saw that uh the control  model with uh certain control codes were  generating more  uh more frequently this kind of  sentences so  uh our dataset was not aimed to  explicitly extract certain kind of  biases it was comprehensive and it was  designed to simulate a real world  situation and so  uh it's a very it was a very alarming  result for us to see this kind of biases  in the model and  moving forward our aim is to study how  these spices propagate in downstream  tasks as well as how we can mitigate the  biases before they are  used with a real world system  thanks to all of that that makes sense  so  i guess to close uh i'm interested in  gathering insights from this group on  what's in store for the future  uh where do you see the ai community  focusing efforts  and how are we tackling this important  topic  um let me give you some  thoughts on this topic um so if you  think of fairness in  ai or responsibly more broadly  it's a very timely challenge for the  society that  we all have the opportunity to address  and  to do research and to work on um so  clearly this is not a challenge  limited to any one company or even  the tech industry as a whole um so  because of this reason we have been now  partnering with the  academic researchers our peer companies  and the broader community  so for example we have been working with  the partnership on ai  to help draft guidelines and best  practices on these topics  uh we have also been um engaging with  the academy through  amazon research awards on related topics  um through internships and also  collaborations with professors  this is something that uh i'm very  excited about to  collaborate even more with the various  professors and their students  so personally uh i'm excited about  being at the intersection of the  academia and industry  um and have been presenting tutorials on  topics like uh privacy industries  fairness  or explainability in industry  responsibility in industry and so on  jointly with collaborators from other  peer companies so that we  share our collective experience present  the  open challenges research challenges that  we are seeing  in the industry in this space and  thereby also help drive collaboration  with the academic researchers  so finally let me uh note that  it's very important to build an  inclusive  and diverse research community on these  topics  so because of the uh significant  societal  implications of these disciplines we  need diversity in terms of  demographics uh lived experience  perspectives  expertise and so on and  uh really thankful that  we have several cross disciplinary  conferences uh such as the  asean conference on fairness  transparency  fairness accountability and transparency  um as well as the  aaa acm conference on the artificial  intelligence ethics and society  where we see researchers not just from  computer science but also from  other disciplines and thereby through  this cross-pollination of research we  are seeing  the right primitives or the right  notions  on these topics starting to emerge this  is what  keeps me really excited about these  areas  yes i'd like to uh second uh krishna i'm  there it is really exciting  and great to see all the acceleration in  fairness research  and uh it's actually making all the way  uh into you know  industrial systems it's having a real uh  real life impact  uh and it's changing how ml systems uh  are built at scale  and we ourselves my team and myself have  benefited from  such a collaboration uh emphasis now  lies in making sure that  the research that happens in the  academic setting quickly finds its way  into production and  the time lag between two is minimized  and as krishna mentioned  there are all these platforms that help  us get connected  uh as as a  researcher you know we benefit a lot  attending some of these conferences  which are specifically focused on  fairness and we ourselves would be  actually organizing  a workshop at north american chapter of  acl and we  uh hope to learn a lot from from that  workshop  i'd also like to point out that a closer  collaboration between industry and  academia has  uh uh paved the way for you know all  this progress to happen  and it's also uh uh an interesting  observation here that  the industry is making inroads uh by  consulting policy makers when it comes  to fairness research and its  implementation so  it's becoming a bigger community we are  getting uh tightly  net more and more and i i am very uh  hopeful about the  uh of uh of this uh this research  because of all this collaboration  i can go um so of course it's uh  hard to predict what things will look  like in five or ten years  but i would urge people to maybe look  beyond the standard  um machine learning conferences and  you know as in any hot field although  there's been a lot of work in fairness  and machine learning  not all of it has been the most uh  thoughtful and coherent  but there is this sort of steady stream  of work that tends to come out of  um things like the theoretical computer  science community  and and in fact there's a whole um  theory conference uh called foundations  of responsible computing that  that i helped start last year and this  is going to continue again this year  which some of this work is happening  that is trying to think about  um let's say more principled approaches  to thinking about fairness  and an obstacle to lots of  it's often the case that these more  principled approaches have  serious obstacles to deployment which um  is why we don't see more of this  um but i think we should remember that  with something like differential privacy  which is now  you know a widely deployed technology  you know it took 10 years for for the  transition from from the whiteboard to  to a real technology and i'm hopeful  that in the next  five or 10 years we'll start to see  payoff from some of this more principled  theoretical research and that some of  these more sort of foundational ideas  will start to to see their way into  practice  yeah so uh then really quickly before we  go uh amazon also has a collaboration  with the nsf on fairness and ai efforts  can you speak to the importance of the  nsf  uh in this space and how it's helping  drive  improvements in fairness and ai  here ashley i can take that question so  actually a year ago amazon  and a national science foundation  announced a 20 million dollar  collaboration  to fund academic research on fairness in  ai over a three year period  so this was a great thing that started  last year the goal of this collaboration  is to promote academic research  and this important aspect of ai our  primary objective for engaging with  academia  on issues related to fairness and  transparency in aia  is to get many different and diverse  perspective focused on the challenge  so uh the the first 10 recipients  uh of grant issued projects at the  itascore broad research areas  the first one was ensuring fairness  algorithms and the system that  incorporate them  which begins with the definition and  quantification of fairness and i guess  we talked about that a little bit uh in  this uh  in this conversation the second one was  accountability and transparency in ai  algorithms  third one using ai to promote equity  in society and finally the fourth one  was ensuring that the benefits of ai  are available to everyone  so what we hope in the future is that  the future cycles of research will build  upon the success of the first round  by bringing an even greater diversity of  perspectives on definition  and perceptions of fairness  wonderful i want to thank you all for  sharing your insights on this important  topic  of fairness and ai for those who have  tuned into the session  we invite you to reach out to us to  discuss our research  efforts or experience working with  amazon  one-on-one i'll also mention you can  read more about  the winning grants and amazon's  collaboration  with the national science foundation as  well as various fairness and ai blog  posts and articles  on amazon.science website  so again thank you all for tuning in  hope you have a good one
 

